  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math> be a random variable such that the probability function of a distribution is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>j</mi></msup></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8734;</mo></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;Then the mean of the distribution and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi></math> is positive and even<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;respectively, are:",
